"Sumerian_city-state": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_%28Stele_of_the_Vultures%29%2C_c._2450_BC_%2828008189230%29.jpg/1280px-Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_%28Stele_of_the_Vultures%29%2C_c._2450_BC_%2828008189230%29.jpg","title":"Sumerian city-state","desc":"Limestone victory stele of Eannatum of Lagash, known as the Stele of the Vultures, c. 2450 BCE, in the Ancient Near East gallery of the Louvre. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_(Stele_of_the_Vultures),_c._2450_BC_(28008189230).jpg","alt":"Three joined limestone fragments forming a broad gable, carved in low relief with helmeted soldiers, fallen bodies and a standing figure beside a tree, over a band of cuneiform signs"},
